  "handoff": {
    "residue": "SV/ML have zero preparation slack and share a detach/couple architecture despite different identities. Their sixth action voluntarily restores H. Model equality establishes no empirical equivalence. C has lower burden and conventional status; all remain viable.",
    "summary": "SUMMARY: Retain J0-C, J0-SV and J0-ML exactly, not combined. Invariant: retention >=4 at ticks 3-6, separation and all retainers removed by 10; one action per tick completes next boundary, resources cannot duplicate, only one non-pin assembly and no pins mixed with it. C: install C tick 0, remove C tick 7, idle otherwise. SV: detach H 0, install V 1, insert S,V 2, withdraw S,V 7, remove V 8, assemble H 9, idle 3-6. ML: detach H 0, place M 1, route L 2, unroute L 7, lift M 8, assemble H 9, idle 3-6. Primitive changes replace tap with direct C capacity or detached-component min(4,4) coupling; H cannot tap detached. Supplied model replay reports each feasible with retention 4 on all hold ticks, zero impacts and no errors. C costs two active ticks, SV/ML six including optional restoration; five suffice for their required function. Available variable is supported construction choice; critique changes no operations. Method mismatch remains: ownership/process exchange and task removal have no source-bound opening. Refuse task deletion, new resources, mixed retainers, elastic extrapolation and invented diversity. Q theta remains {0,4} unmeasured; analog sources add no retention. Keep all viable, preserve SV/ML tie; lower C burden is established within this comparison, not global optimality. No world execution, universal novelty or canonical skill-executor claim."
  },
